<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1290–<a href="/years/1346">1346</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili was an Andalusi poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> who accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> in the 1320s. Tradition credits him with designing buildings for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>, including a royal audience chamber and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and died at Timbuktu in 1346.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was also known as al-Tuwayjin, the small casserole, a name no one has explained, and his nisba al-Sahili, from his maternal grandfather, points to a family of the coast. His father Muhammad, trained in jurisprudence, headed the perfume <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Granada al-Sahili drafted legal documents and became known as a poet, praised by his contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a>.</p><p>A near-contemporary poet claimed that, under the influence of a badly prepared marking nut, he once fell into a temporary madness and declared himself a prophet. For whatever reason, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in disgrace in about 1321 and travelled through Mamluk Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Jalayirid Iraq and Rasulid Yemen before making the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> in 1324.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a>, who enjoyed his conversation and took him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, where he became the best known of the scholars who came to the empire after the pilgrimage. He directed the building of an audience chamber in the capital, for which Musa paid him 12,000 mithqals of gold, a sum that may have included the construction budget; his own part may have been largely managerial, though his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> was admired and he may have decorated the building.</p><p>He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, where in 1334 he hosted the Alexandrian merchant Siraj al-Din, who died in his house. Between 1331 and 1337 he travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and exchanged gifts with the Marinid sultan Abu al-Hasan, and was attacked by bandits on his way back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Sahili died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> on 15 October 1346 and was buried there; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> saw his grave in 1353. Many modern sources call him an architect and credit him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a>, a palace in Timbuktu and the mosque of Gao, and Maurice Delafosse saw him as the creator of Sudano-Sahelian architecture. The sources support only the audience chamber. Labelle Prussin has described that tradition as a centuries-long synthesis of West African and Islamic design rather than one foreigner's import.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Sahili” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Timbuktu and Djenné became centres of learning and manuscript production, attracting scholars, merchants and pilgrims, and Mansa Musa is credited with commissioning the Djinguereber mosque in Timbuktu, traditionally attributed to the Andalusi poet-architect <b>Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mansa Musa brought scholars back from his pilgrimage, among them Maliki jurists and the Andalusi poet <b>Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</b>; the great mosque of Timbuktu, the Djinguereber, was built under him in 1327, and he sent West African scholars to study at Fez.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1326" aria-label="Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abu Ishaq al-Sahili"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4755 4384h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4755 4384h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1326">What was here in 1326? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
